  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Seif Ples Centre is a clinic and referral centre providing first response essential medical assistance, temporary accommodation and support services to survivors/victims of Gender Base Violence (GBV) and Family Sexual Violence (FSV). Australia provides support to Seif Ples through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Justice (ASIPJ).  The support to Seif Ples aligns with the Family Protection Act effort and to support our justice partners to become increasingly sensitive and responsive to the needs of women and others vulnerable to gender base violence and family &amp; sexual violence.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Gender equality</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Justice (ASIPJ), Australia has supported gender equality, disability, and social inclusion (GEDSI) outcomes, particularly to support Solomon Islands Government Justice agencies in their effort to implement the Family Protection Act (FPA) 2014. In 2024-25, a priority of ASIPJ&#x2019;s GEDSI approach is continued support for the &#x2018;Step by Step 2022-2024 implementation of the Justice sector&#x2019;s first Gender Equality and Social Inclusion strategy. In relation to services provided to victim/survivors of sexual and gender-based violence, Justice Information Management System (JIMS) statistics demonstrated provision of services by relevant Justice agencies in 2024. For example, implementation of the Office of the Director Public Prosecutions (ODPP&#x2019;s) revised Family and Sexual Offenses (FASO) guidelines and links to ODPP open files and prosecution of family and sexual offences. Support for court circuits also enabled hearings and rulings on cases under the FPA, the amended Sexual Offences Act 2016, and relevant Penal Code provisions. Strong examples of increased agency sensitivity and responsiveness to the needs of women, girls and others vulnerable to the Family Sexual Violence continue to emerge. For instance, Magistrates Court&#x2019;s updated Bench Book (to include FPA guidance and resolutions) seeks to improve accessibility of FPA protection orders.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the ASIPJ, Australia provided technical support to MJLA, assisting the Attorney General Chambers to consolidate and digitalise legislation and assist with drafting Solomon Islands&#x2019; policies and laws. The project to consolidate and digitise 224 of Solomon Islands law was progressed over seven years. In the reporting period, with the support of Australia, SIG Parliament passed and gazetted a suite of three enabling laws to implement the consolidation and digitisation project &#x2013; the Legislation Act 2023, Constitution (Amendment and Validation 2023) and Legislation Amendment, Repeal and Revalidation 2023, which were all passed in 2023. This legislation enables the Attorney General to reprint, amend, correct and validate laws where necessary, and enable SIG laws to be reproduced without the need for expensive and lengthy Cabinet and Parliament processes. The ability to freely access current, accurate laws is essential for the rule and law and access to justice for all Solomon Islanders.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthening leadership</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Justice (ASIPJ), Australia has supported leadership development across various law and justice agencies in Solomon Islands. This includes investment in formal training and qualifications for legal drafting and correctional services staff, and ongoing Continuing Legal Education and training for government lawyers in Solomon Islands through Twinning Programs and exchanges with Australian institutions, such as the Victorian Bar. Through a combination of formal qualifications, on the job training and learning exchange, Australia is supporting the development of law and justice sector leaders.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Justice (ASIPJ). Australia has provided technical support to strengthen the Solomon Islands justice sector. This includes assisting ministries and agencies in using the Justice Information Management System (JIMS), enhancing data-driven decision making and expanding services like e-Filing System. ASIPJ also supported the Bilateral Melanesian Twinning initiative that helped justice agencies improve planning by adopting successful regional practices and strengthen cultural and institutional ties with counterparts in PNG, Fiji and Vanuatu. Similar collaborative models also exist regionally, involving other Pacific countries and Australian counterparts. ASIPJ also backed legislative reform efforts by supporting the Attorney Generals Chambers (AGC) to digitise and consolidate laws, boosting drafting capacity through mentoring and training. The Legal Digital Reprint and Consolidation Project led to reprinting hundreds of Acts and greater public access to legislation. Additionally, the &#x201c;Fabric of Laws&#x201d; initiative engaged 24 government ministries to clarify legal mandates. In 2024, ASIPJ supported the Ministry of Justice and Legal Affairs (MJLA) in leading a major review of five justice agency mandates, resulting in Cabinet submissions. This marks the most significant constitutional reform since independence and builds on earlier work from the Judicial Autonomy project with the national Judiciary.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Justice, Australia provided technical support to the Attorney General&#x2019;s Chambers to consolidate and digitalise 224 laws over seven years. In the reporting period, Parliament passed a suite of three enabling laws &#x2013; the Legislation Act 2023, Constitution (Amendment and Validation) Act 2023 and the Legislation Amendment, Repeal and Revalidation Act 2023. This legislation enables the Attorney General to reprint, amend, correct and validate laws where necessary, and enables laws to be reproduced without the need for expensive and lengthy Cabinet and Parliament processes. The ability to freely access current, accurate laws is essential for the rule and law and access to justice for all Solomon Islanders. Previously, such access was not possible without travelling to Honiara or accessing a printed copy. This project will enable people in rural and remote areas, or who have accessibility issues, to access current, accurate laws freely online.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
